TITLE:: Deferred
summary:: A promise-like container for values that will be available in the future
categories:: Scheduling
related:: Classes/Condition, Classes/Routine

DESCRIPTION::
Deferred is a promise-like object that represents a value which may not yet be available. It allows you to write asynchronous code in a synchronous style by suspending a thread until a value (or error) is provided.

A Deferred can be in one of three states:
LIST::
## strong::unresolved:: - no value or error has been set yet
## strong::value:: - a value has been set
## strong::error:: - an error has been set
::

Once resolved (either with a value or an error), a Deferred cannot be changed.

The simplest usage is to create a Deferred, pass it to an asynchronous operation, and then wait for the result:

code::
(
fork {
	var d = Deferred();

	// resolve it from another thread after a delay
	{ d.value = 42 }.defer(0.5);

	// this suspends the current thread until the value is set
	d.wait.postln; // -> 42
};
)
::

link::#*using:: is the most common way to create a Deferred. It runs a function in a Routine and resolves the Deferred with the return value (or any thrown error):

code::
(
fork {
	var d = Deferred.using {
		1.0.wait;
		"done"
	};

	d.wait.postln; // -> "done" (after 1 second)
};
)
::

Deferred supports chaining via link::#-then::, which returns a new Deferred whose value is the result of a callback applied to the first Deferred's value:

code::
(
fork {
	var result = Deferred.using { 10 }
		.then { |v| v * 2 }
		.then { |v| v + 1 };

	result.wait.postln; // -> 21
};
)
::

Any object can be converted to an already-resolved Deferred via code::asDeferred:::

code::
100.asDeferred.wait; // -> 100
::

SUBSECTION:: Extension methods

Deferred also provides convenience methods on several core classes to wrap common asynchronous server operations. These return a Deferred that resolves when the server confirms the operation is complete:

code::
(
fork {
	Server.default.doBoot.wait;

	SynthDef(\test, { |out, freq=440|
		Out.ar(out, SinOsc.ar(freq) * 0.1 ! 2)
	}).doAdd.wait;

	var synth = Synth.doNew(\test, [\freq, 880]).wait;
	// synth is guaranteed to be running on the server

	1.0.wait;
	synth.free;
};
)
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
Create a new unresolved Deferred.

code::
d = Deferred();
::

METHOD:: using
Create a new Deferred and resolve it with the return value of code::func::. If code::func:: throws an error, the Deferred is resolved with that error. If code::func:: returns another Deferred, waits for that Deferred's value.

ARGUMENT:: func
A function to execute. Receives the Deferred as its first argument.

ARGUMENT:: clock
Clock to run the function on. Defaults to the current thread's clock.

code::
(
fork {
	var d = Deferred.using { |d|
		0.5.wait;
		"hello"
	};
	d.wait.postln; // -> "hello"
};
)
::

METHOD:: serverSync
Create a Deferred that runs code::func::, then waits for the server to complete all pending async commands before resolving. Useful for operations that allocate server-side resources.

ARGUMENT:: func
A function to execute.

ARGUMENT:: server
Server to sync with. Defaults to code::Server.default::.

code::
(
fork {
	Deferred.serverSync {
		SynthDef(\test, { Out.ar(0, SinOsc.ar(440) * 0.1) }).add;
	}.wait;
	// SynthDef is guaranteed to be on the server
};
)
::

METHOD:: newResolved
Create a Deferred that is already resolved with the given value.

ARGUMENT:: value
The value to resolve with.

code::
Deferred.newResolved(42).wait; // -> 42
::


INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: wait
Suspend the current thread until this Deferred is resolved. Returns the value if resolved with a value, or throws the error if resolved with an error.

ARGUMENT:: timeout
Optional timeout in seconds. If the Deferred is not resolved within this time, a link::Classes/DeferredTimeoutError:: is thrown.

code::
(
fork {
	var d = Deferred();
	{ d.value = "result" }.defer(0.1);
	d.wait.postln; // -> "result"
};
)

// with timeout
(
fork {
	var d = Deferred();
	try {
		d.wait(0.5);
	} {
		|err|
		err.postln; // -> DeferredTimeoutError
	};
};
)
::

METHOD:: value
When used as a getter, returns the resolved value. Throws link::Classes/AccessingDeferredValueError:: if unresolved, or re-throws the error if resolved with an error.

When used as a setter, resolves the Deferred with the given value and resumes all waiting threads. Throws link::Classes/ResettingDeferredValueError:: if already resolved.

ARGUMENT:: inValue
The value to resolve with (setter only).

code::
(
var d = Deferred();
d.value = 42;
d.value.postln; // -> 42
)
::

METHOD:: error
When used as a getter, returns the stored error (or nil if none).

When used as a setter, resolves the Deferred with an error and resumes all waiting threads. Any thread that calls code::wait:: will have this error thrown. Throws link::Classes/ResettingDeferredValueError:: if already resolved.

ARGUMENT:: inError
The error to resolve with (setter only).

code::
(
fork {
	var d = Deferred();
	{ d.error = Error("something went wrong") }.defer(0.1);
	try {
		d.wait;
	} {
		|err|
		err.errorString.postln;
	};
};
)
::

METHOD:: then
Register callbacks that are called when the Deferred resolves. Returns a new Deferred that resolves with the return value of the callback.

If the Deferred resolves with a value, code::valueFunc:: is called. If it resolves with an error, code::errorFunc:: is called. If code::errorFunc:: returns a non-Exception value, the returned Deferred resolves with that value (error recovery). If it returns an Exception, the returned Deferred resolves with that error.

ARGUMENT:: valueFunc
Function called with the resolved value. Defaults to the identity function.

ARGUMENT:: errorFunc
Function called with the error. Defaults to re-throwing the error.

ARGUMENT:: clock
Clock to run callbacks on. Defaults to the current thread's clock.

ARGUMENT:: timeout
Optional timeout in seconds for waiting on this Deferred.

code::
(
fork {
	// chaining
	var result = Deferred.using { 10 }
		.then { |v| v * 2 }
		.then { |v| v + 5 };
	result.wait.postln; // -> 25
};
)

// error handling
(
fork {
	var result = Deferred.using { Error("oops").throw }
		.then(
			valueFunc: { |v| "got: %".format(v) },
			errorFunc: { |e| "recovered" }   // recover from error
		);
	result.wait.postln; // -> "recovered"
};
)
::

METHOD:: onValue
Register a callback that fires only when the Deferred resolves with a value. Does not return a new Deferred.

ARGUMENT:: function
Function called with the resolved value.

ARGUMENT:: clock
Clock to run the callback on.

code::
(
var d = Deferred();
d.onValue { |v| "got: %".format(v).postln };
d.value = 42; // prints "got: 42"
)
::

METHOD:: onError
Register a callback that fires only when the Deferred resolves with an error. Does not return a new Deferred.

ARGUMENT:: function
Function called with the error.

ARGUMENT:: clock
Clock to run the callback on.

METHOD:: using
Run a function and resolve this Deferred with its return value (or any thrown error). If the function returns another Deferred, waits for that Deferred to resolve first.

ARGUMENT:: function
The function to execute. Receives this Deferred as its argument.

ARGUMENT:: clock
Clock to run on. Defaults to the current thread's clock.

ARGUMENT:: fork
If true, always forks a new thread. If false (default), uses code::forkIfNeeded:: which reuses the current thread if already on the correct clock.

METHOD:: done
Post the resolved value to the post window, or throw the error if resolved with an error. Useful for debugging.

METHOD:: valueCallback
Returns a function that, when called with a value, resolves this Deferred. Useful for bridging callback-based APIs.

code::
(
var d = Deferred();
var callback = d.valueCallback;
// pass callback to some async API
callback.value(42);
d.value.postln; // -> 42
)
::

METHOD:: errorCallback
Returns a function that, when called with an error, resolves this Deferred with that error. Useful for bridging callback-based APIs.

METHOD:: completionMsg
Generate an OSC completion message (code::\sync::) that resolves this Deferred when the server sends its reply. The resolved value is the elapsed time in seconds.

ARGUMENT:: server
Server to listen on. Defaults to code::Server.default::.

returns:: An OSC message array code::[\sync, id]:: suitable for use as a completion message.

METHOD:: hasValue
returns:: code::true:: if this Deferred has been resolved with a value.

METHOD:: hasError
returns:: code::true:: if this Deferred has been resolved with an error.

METHOD:: isResolved
returns:: code::true:: if this Deferred has been resolved (with either a value or an error).

METHOD:: asDeferred
returns:: code::this::. Allows Deferred to be used polymorphically with code::Object:asDeferred::.

METHOD:: dump
Post a summary of this Deferred's state to the post window, including resolution status, value/error, and the number of waiting threads.


EXAMPLES::

SUBSECTION:: Basic usage

code::
// Create, resolve, and wait
(
fork {
	var d = Deferred();
	{ d.value = "hello" }.defer(0.5);
	d.wait.postln; // -> "hello" (after 0.5 seconds)
};
)
::

SUBSECTION:: Deferred.using with error handling

code::
(
fork {
	var d = Deferred.using {
		0.2.wait;
		if(0.5.coin) {
			"success"
		} {
			Error("failure").throw
		};
	};

	try {
		d.wait.postln;
	} {
		|err|
		"Caught: %".format(err.errorString).postln;
	};
};
)
::

SUBSECTION:: Chaining with then

code::
(
fork {
	var result = Deferred.using { 5 }
		.then { |v| v * 10 }
		.then { |v| v.asString ++ " items" };

	result.wait.postln; // -> "50 items"
};
)
::

SUBSECTION:: Bridging callback APIs with valueCallback

code::
(
fork {
	var buf, d = Deferred();
	// Buffer.read uses a callback - bridge it with valueCallback
	Buffer.read(s, Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav",
		action: d.valueCallback);
	buf = d.wait;
	"Loaded buffer: % frames, % channels".format(
		buf.numFrames, buf.numChannels
	).postln;
};
)
::

SUBSECTION:: Timeout

code::
(
fork {
	var d = Deferred();
	// never resolved — will timeout
	try {
		d.wait(1.0);
	} {
		|err|
		"Timed out: %".format(err.errorString).postln;
	};
};
)
::

SUBSECTION:: Nested Deferreds

If a code::using:: function returns another Deferred, the outer Deferred waits for the inner one to resolve:

code::
(
fork {
	var d = Deferred.using {
		0.1.wait;
		// return an inner Deferred
		Deferred.using {
			0.1.wait;
			"nested result"
		}
	};
	d.wait.postln; // -> "nested result"
};
)
::
